WEBVTT

00:00.060 --> 00:01.500
Instructor: So the first thing we're going to cover

00:01.500 --> 00:02.790
is cloning.

00:02.790 --> 00:04.380
Cloning is a really important part

00:04.380 --> 00:06.720
of working with remote repositories.

00:06.720 --> 00:09.600
In fact, it's such an important thing that we do in Git.

00:09.600 --> 00:12.510
If we go to the documentation under reference,

00:12.510 --> 00:14.430
all the commands are grouped together

00:14.430 --> 00:16.560
like the basic snapshot and commands:

00:16.560 --> 00:20.790
adding and git status, git commits, branching and merging.

00:20.790 --> 00:21.990
They're grouped together.

00:21.990 --> 00:25.950
But then up top there are two commands side by side,

00:25.950 --> 00:30.000
getting and creating projects: inits and clone.

00:30.000 --> 00:31.680
So we've seen how to use git init

00:31.680 --> 00:34.350
to create a new project or a new repo from scratch.

00:34.350 --> 00:36.750
We've done that for every repository

00:36.750 --> 00:39.090
but git clone does something different.

00:39.090 --> 00:42.360
Git clone actually goes and gets a repository

00:42.360 --> 00:44.190
that is not on your machine

00:44.190 --> 00:46.680
and it brings it to your machine.

00:46.680 --> 00:49.410
It basically downloads the contents of a repository

00:49.410 --> 00:52.470
based upon some URL that we provide.

00:52.470 --> 00:56.970
Typically, that URL will be from a service like GitHub.

00:56.970 --> 00:58.020
And in this course

00:58.020 --> 01:01.770
and 99% of the open source projects out there,

01:01.770 --> 01:04.170
it will be a GitHub repository that you're cloning

01:04.170 --> 01:05.880
but it could be something else.

01:05.880 --> 01:09.570
Bitbucket, it could be a self-hosted repository.

01:09.570 --> 01:11.310
Git does not care.

01:11.310 --> 01:13.350
The command git clone is part of Git.

01:13.350 --> 01:15.600
It's not part of GitHub.

01:15.600 --> 01:18.960
So here it is git clone and then a URL

01:18.960 --> 01:22.290
and that URL is where Git will go and retrieve

01:22.290 --> 01:25.380
all the relevant information for a repository from.

01:25.380 --> 01:27.540
It will copy the files to your machine.

01:27.540 --> 01:30.900
It initializes a new repository on your machine

01:30.900 --> 01:33.330
so that gives you access to the full Git history

01:33.330 --> 01:35.100
of whatever project you're cloning

01:35.100 --> 01:37.590
and I'm gonna demo this in just a moment.

01:37.590 --> 01:40.980
So as an example, there's some repository on GitHub.

01:40.980 --> 01:44.400
Let's say it's, I don't know, it's React

01:44.400 --> 01:46.500
or it's a "Flappy Bird" clone.

01:46.500 --> 01:49.140
It's some game on GitHub.

01:49.140 --> 01:51.660
I want the source code and the Git history.

01:51.660 --> 01:55.530
So if I run git clone with the corresponding URL,

01:55.530 --> 01:59.070
whatever that GitHub URL is for that hosted repo,

01:59.070 --> 02:01.020
git is gonna go fetch all that

02:01.020 --> 02:03.150
and bring it down to my machine.

02:03.150 --> 02:06.690
I'll have the full history, all the commits, all the files.

02:06.690 --> 02:09.180
So I'll go ahead and demonstrate this.

02:09.180 --> 02:12.750
Here's a GitHub repository that I don't have.

02:12.750 --> 02:17.103
It's for the game "2048" which some of you probably know.

02:18.240 --> 02:20.430
This game, very addictive.

02:20.430 --> 02:23.040
It's, I don't know how many years old now but anyway,

02:23.040 --> 02:26.760
it's just a JavaScript, HTML, some CSS,

02:26.760 --> 02:27.780
that it's a game.

02:27.780 --> 02:30.030
You can open it up and play it in your browser.

02:30.030 --> 02:31.440
So if I want the code for that

02:31.440 --> 02:33.300
and I want the full Git history,

02:33.300 --> 02:35.610
of course I can see it on GitHub, right?

02:35.610 --> 02:38.250
That's what GitHub is doing for me here as a user.

02:38.250 --> 02:40.170
I can view this URL.

02:40.170 --> 02:42.540
I can look at different commits, 180 commits.

02:42.540 --> 02:44.100
I can see who contributed.

02:44.100 --> 02:45.360
I can read about it.

02:45.360 --> 02:47.010
I can take a look at, you know,

02:47.010 --> 02:50.010
the index HTML file in the browser.

02:50.010 --> 02:52.530
But I'm gonna clone this URL.

02:52.530 --> 02:55.110
One option is just to copy this URL right here.

02:55.110 --> 02:57.570
Another option on GitHub is to click right here

02:57.570 --> 03:00.330
and it's gonna show me the clone URL

03:00.330 --> 03:02.040
which is the same thing as we see up there.

03:02.040 --> 03:03.330
I can click this button.

03:03.330 --> 03:04.710
I can, you know, Command+C,

03:04.710 --> 03:06.630
however you want to get that URL.

03:06.630 --> 03:08.340
I'm gonna get that URL.

03:08.340 --> 03:09.173
And by the way,

03:09.173 --> 03:11.790
this repository link is in the resources with this video.

03:11.790 --> 03:15.030
If you wanna try this, definitely recommend you do.

03:15.030 --> 03:17.190
Now, I'm gonna go over to my terminal.

03:17.190 --> 03:19.170
The first thing you always wanna do when you're cloning

03:19.170 --> 03:21.077
is make sure that you're not in a repository.

03:21.077 --> 03:23.190
Just like when you run git init.

03:23.190 --> 03:25.320
So I just like to do git status.

03:25.320 --> 03:27.750
Okay, we're not in a repository.

03:27.750 --> 03:29.850
And then wherever you run git clone,

03:29.850 --> 03:33.480
that is the place, that folder, where git is going to create

03:33.480 --> 03:35.070
a new folder for you.

03:35.070 --> 03:38.610
So I'm in a folder called what is this?

03:38.610 --> 03:42.120
Just, well actually, I meant to be in this folder Cloning.

03:42.120 --> 03:43.890
So now I'm in a folder Cloning.

03:43.890 --> 03:45.120
There's nothing in here.

03:45.120 --> 03:47.850
It's gonna make me a new folder when I run this command

03:47.850 --> 03:50.880
git clone and then I paste the URL

03:50.880 --> 03:52.560
that's coming from GitHub.

03:52.560 --> 03:54.900
I don't have anything from this URL.

03:54.900 --> 03:57.390
I don't have this repository on this machine.

03:57.390 --> 03:58.743
I'm not in a git repo.

03:59.580 --> 04:01.110
But when that finishes

04:01.110 --> 04:04.620
and you will need internet for this, when that finishes,

04:04.620 --> 04:07.080
we see a new folder that was not there before.

04:07.080 --> 04:09.270
I typed LS, there was nothing.

04:09.270 --> 04:13.050
If I go into that folder and I type LS,

04:13.050 --> 04:15.690
okay I've got a whole bunch of stuff, right?

04:15.690 --> 04:20.430
I've got index.html, JavaScript folder, style folder,

04:20.430 --> 04:22.230
some other stuff going on.

04:22.230 --> 04:26.070
And it looks like this is a git repo, right?

04:26.070 --> 04:29.100
If I type git status, yes it is.

04:29.100 --> 04:32.943
If I type git log, I can see the full history of commits.

04:33.810 --> 04:35.310
See all that stuff.

04:35.310 --> 04:37.560
That's all or it came from GitHub.

04:37.560 --> 04:39.690
I can see all the commits and who made them,

04:39.690 --> 04:40.773
when they were made.

04:41.670 --> 04:43.230
There's, you know, all the code here.

04:43.230 --> 04:45.330
I can open it up and take a look.

04:45.330 --> 04:47.310
I can even run this.

04:47.310 --> 04:49.440
And there, this is actually running.

04:49.440 --> 04:50.820
This is not the hosted version.

04:50.820 --> 04:51.960
This is running from my machine.

04:51.960 --> 04:54.570
You can see here and there we are.

04:54.570 --> 04:56.400
I could play around with it myself.

04:56.400 --> 04:58.140
I could change the styles.

04:58.140 --> 05:01.770
